   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <p>With Xmas greetings from us both and best wishes for New Year. <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">W i P is n. i. ni</seg>. Moi aussi.</p>                                            	<closer>
                        <signed>
                            <name type="person" key="joyc82">James Joyce</name>
                        </signed>
                        <dateline>
                            <date when="1938-12-25">Xmas ’38</date>
                        </dateline>
                    </closer>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">At the end of 1938, JJ was still guarded about the title of the forthcoming <name type="work" key="W_FINNEGANSWAKE">Finnegans Wake</name>, referring to it as “Work in Progress” until early 1939. Yet in December of 1938 he considered the book to be finished; Paul Léon wrote on JJ’s behalf to Constantine Curran on <rs type="letter" key="L_71857">26 December 1938</rs> (the following day) that W.i.P “is finished”. During December and January JJ had been correcting proofs for the book, of which he received the first copy at the end of January. As he was composing Christmas greetings to the Budgen family, he added that “W i P is n. i. ni” in the form of the phrase “U. p: up” (which appears some dozen times in <name type="work" key="W_ULYSSES">Ulysses</name>, the first at <name type="work" key="W_ULYSSES">U</name> 8.258): in other words, writing in French that his new book is finished. The French “n i, ni, c'est fini” was in colloquial use since the end of the 18th century as a closing phrase to state that something has stopped or is finished. The French sentence “Moi aussi” (“Me too”) signaled relief: “I’m exhausted, done for, dead”.</note>
            </div>
      </body>
